# 65-DAY AI-ASSISTED DEVELOPMENT COST AUDIT — WINDOW B
## GPT audit companion to the original 30-day / ~$53k Window A assessment

**Audit window:** approximately 18 July 2026 through 21 September 2026  
**Calendar length:** approximately 65 days  
**Creator active time:** approximately 140 hours  
**Direct AI subscription spend used for the episode comparison:** approximately $60  
**Primary audit lens:** professional replacement labor at the same wage-equivalent style used for Window A  
**Audit subject:** work actually performed during Window B only

---

# EXECUTIVE SUMMARY

This audit estimates the professional labor that would be required to reproduce the work actually performed during the second development window.

It is intentionally **not**:

- a company valuation;
- a resale valuation;
- an intellectual-property valuation;
- a claim that every project is complete;
- a claim that publication dates equal project start dates;
- a claim that AI worked without human direction;
- a claim that all output is public;
- or a reason to bill work from Window A a second time.

The main result is:

```text
WINDOW B
approximately 2,280–3,740 professional replacement hours

same wage-equivalent lens as Window A:
approximately $130,000–$214,000

midpoint:
approximately $172,000
```

For comparison, the previously audited Window A was:

```text
WINDOW A
approximately 948 professional replacement hours
approximately $52,970 wage-equivalent
approximately $20 AI subscription cost
approximately 140 creator-active hours
```

Window A is treated as a **closed baseline**.

The Window B total therefore counts only new work, later integration work, later upgrades, later QA/release work, and incomplete research that was actually implemented or tested during Window B.

---

# THE QUESTION THIS AUDIT ANSWERS

The useful question is not:

> "What are these repositories worth?"

The useful question is:

> "If competent professionals had to reproduce the implementation, testing, debugging, release preparation, documentation, integration, and support work that was actually completed during this 65-day period, how much professional labor would that represent?"

That is the same general question used in the first assessment.

The answer is expressed first in **replacement hours**, then translated into a **wage-equivalent** using approximately the same role rates as the earlier audit.

---

# CLOSED BASELINE — WINDOW A

The original 30-day assessment already counted the following work families and therefore they are **not rebuilt from zero inside Window B**:

- CryptoChess / Crypto.Chess
- PQC Scout-Knife
- SPX-QEC and associated PQC work already inside the first audit
- PQCAssets / PQC Containers already included in the first period
- Bitninja Mocap Lite as a standalone product
- Deck Card Widget / original 3DCP controller work
- SWAR original reader/editor
- Streamer Board & Console original application
- G502V repair work
- Soundcard launcher/fix
- the cross-application documentation/grouping work included in the first audit
- the first audit itself

Window A published total:

```text
replacement labor: ~948 hours
wage-equivalent:   ~$52,970
AI seat cost:      ~$20
creator time:      ~140 hours
```

The rule for Window B is therefore:

```text
OLD PRODUCT FROM WINDOW A
        +
NEW BUGFIX / NEW INTEGRATION / NEW RELEASE WORK IN WINDOW B
        =
COUNT ONLY THE NEW LAYER
```

The original product is not charged twice.

---

# WINDOW B INPUTS

Approximate episode accounting:

```text
calendar:
~65 days

creator-active time:
~140 hours

AI subscription cost:
~$60 total across the approximately two-month period

working pattern:
casual / intermittent rather than the concentrated Window A schedule
```

The creator-active-hours figure is not used as the labor estimate.

It records time spent in the human loop: planning, prompting, reviewing, testing, running terminal commands, rejecting incorrect output, reporting bugs, making design decisions, packaging releases, and deciding what to build next.

The **replacement-hour estimate** asks a separate question:

> How much competent professional effort would be needed to reproduce the performed work?

---

# PRIMARY WAGE-EQUIVALENT METHOD

To keep Window B comparable to the original assessment, this audit uses approximately the same role values:

```text
coding / engineering:
~$64 per professional hour

QA / verification:
~$49 per professional hour

documentation / process / coaching:
~$44 per professional hour
```

No workstream is treated as 100% coding.

Each project contains some combination of:

- implementation;
- architecture;
- debugging;
- test construction;
- regression testing;
- manual verification;
- release preparation;
- installer/update work;
- documentation;
- user support;
- environment setup;
- integration;
- and project-management/process work.

The listed dollar values therefore use a rough blended role cost appropriate to each workstream.

These are deliberately estimates, not time-clock records from hired employees.

