# WHY THE GROK CONTRACTOR ESTIMATE LANDED CLOSE

An earlier outside-model pass estimated Window B at roughly:

```text
$200k–$370k contractor-shaped
```

That range is surprisingly close to this audit's simple $100/hour contractor lens:

```text
$228k–$374k
```

However, the inventories were not identical.

The earlier public-view pass placed a larger share of the work burden on SignalCloud and missed or undercounted work that was not obvious from the public surface, including portions of:

- Icon Forge;
- avatar/VRM work;
- Hood Rat Brawler;
- Quantum Warehouse;
- TBPR/MRPS;
- TimeLedger;
- Linux/GitHub/terminal coaching.

The numerical similarity should therefore not be interpreted as two independent audits proving every row exactly.

It means that different assumptions happened to converge on a similar contractor-scale range.

---

# WHAT THIS AUDIT SAYS ABOUT MODEL SPECIALIZATION

Window B used a different workflow from "give every task to one model."

The practical division used during the period can be summarized as:

```text
research / outside-world reconciliation
        ↓
structured packet / plan
        ↓
code construction
        ↓
human test
        ↓
bug report / constrained next slice
        ↓
release / documentation / verification
```

The episode's model-routing idea is that different model seats can be used for the jobs where they are strongest rather than repeatedly paying one expensive seat to research, architect, code, review, document, and glue every task.

The observed Window B record is **consistent with** that workflow being useful.

It does not prove, by itself, that model specialization caused the entire increase over Window A.

Other factors changed too:

- the work mix changed;
- some foundations already existed;
- the creator had more experience with the workflow;
- reusable test/release patterns accumulated;
- some projects could inherit tools or concepts from earlier work;
- Window B had a longer calendar;
- project difficulty was not identical.

Therefore the defensible claim is:

> Routing research, planning, coding, review, and glue work to different tools was part of a workflow that produced a much larger audited replacement-work inventory during Window B at low direct subscription cost.

The stronger causal sentence—

> specialization alone created the entire difference—

is not supported by this audit.

---

# SUBSCRIPTION COST IS NOT TOTAL COST

A $60 subscription figure is useful for describing direct cash spent on the AI seats.

It is **not** the total economic cost of the work.

The user still supplied:

- approximately 140 active hours;
- product ideas;
- design decisions;
- acceptance/rejection decisions;
- hands-on testing;
- screenshots;
- terminal output;
- bug reports;
- release decisions;
- hardware and electricity;
- prior knowledge;
- existing project foundations.

So this audit should not be summarized as:

```text
"$60 created $172,000 from nothing."
```

A more accurate summary is:

```text
"Approximately $60 in AI subscriptions,
combined with about 140 hours of active human direction/testing
and existing project foundations,
was used to perform work whose professional replacement labor
is estimated at about $130k–$214k on the same wage-equivalent lens
as the earlier ~$53k audit."
```

---

# UNCERTAINTY

This is an estimation exercise.

The uncertainty band exists because professional teams can differ dramatically in:

- familiarity with the stack;
- debugging speed;
- QA standards;
- architecture choices;
- release process;
- documentation standards;
- reuse of existing libraries;
- accessibility experience;
- Linux/Wayland experience;
- blockchain familiarity;
- research background.

The hour bands are therefore more defensible than a single exact hour total.

Likewise, the rounded episode figure:

```text
$130k–$214k
```

is preferable to presenting:

```text
$172,100
```

as though it were an invoice.

---

